<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">February Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The February Revolution of 1917 was the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> that forced the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> monarchy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, establishing a provisional government that was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/february-revolution.jpg" alt="February Revolution" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jones, Stinton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manifestaci%C3%B3nAFavorDeLaRep%C3%BAblicaPetrogrado1917--russiainrevolut00jone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>the February Revolution</b> of 1917 the Russian Provisional Government created the Autonomous Governorate of Estonia, uniting the Estonian-speaking districts of the old Estonian and Livonian governorates, and an elected provincial assembly, the Maapäev, met in Tallinn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained the anthem until the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a></li><li><blockquote>In Russia, Peter Lavrov wrote new Russian words to the melody in 1875; this &quot;Worker's Marseillaise&quot; was sung in the Revolution of 1905 and served as the semi-official anthem of the Russian republic after the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b>, Russia's Provisional Government decreed on 12 April 1917 that the Governorate of Estonia would be joined with the Estonian-speaking northern districts of the Governorate of Livonia, uniting the Estonian people in one autonomous province.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917 Bashkir nationalists led by Ahmet-Zaki Validi (Zeki Velidi Togan) held congresses in Orenburg and Ufa.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashkurdistan">Bashkurdistan</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b>, Crimean Tatars organised to win self-government.</blockquote><a href="/state/crimean-peoples-republic">Crimean People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
